{"kind":"request","text":"PB-1: Pinboard adopts the live level for presence. Hugh, 21 November: the map application in the sibling checkout dap-foldkit already proves the live level, and Pinboard in the sibling checkout dap-ui-sdk adopts it also.\nToday Pinboard keeps presence (each participant's name and viewport rectangle) in a hand-written server module: an in-memory map, a five-second expiry timer and its own event stream. Its notes already live in a dap scope. The outcome: presence is a live dap scope declared beside the board definition, and the hand-written presence server is removed.\nStep 1, this request: a design, written as a plan file in the Pinboard checkout, and reported here in one act. No source before my ruling. This request has no landing in this repository; do not write about Pinboard in dap's notes.\n1. Read the map application's live definition, its presence client and its plan section on presence, and state what Pinboard reuses unchanged, what it adapts, and what the two would share if it sat in the client package.\n2. The live definition for Pinboard: the viewport item, its acts, the lease and the timed rule, with bounds.\n3. Identity: a Pinboard tab is an unsigned session with a typed name today, and a live act is a signed intent by a member. State how a participant becomes a member, or what the smallest honest alternative is.\n4. Creation and reads: which scope creates the live child, and how the browser reads and watches it. Give the requests per open tab per hour.\n5. Every gap in dap that the adoption meets, each as a separate finding with file and line. I file those as requests here.\n6. The check: two browsers on one board see each other's viewport move and see a closed tab go within the lease.\nThis lane runs beside the gates and takes no gate slot.","body":{"conditions":"One report act that answers the six points and names the plan file; no source change before the planner's ruling.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef